@page "/learn/tempdata-ssr"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>TempData · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">TempData</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You save an order and go back to the list. How does the list know to say "saved"?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Pages &amp; navigation · Static SSR · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> Blazor components get TempData, a way to pass a value, such as a "saved" message, to the next page after a redirect.
        MVC and Razor Pages always had it. In .NET 10 components didn't, so you put the message in the URL or wrote
        your own cookie.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is an <i>Orders</i> list. Open an order, add a note and save. You land back on the list, which should
        say "Order … saved" once. These pages are static SSR: the server builds each page and remembers nothing
        between requests.
    </p>
    <p>
        This version uses the quick .NET 10 trick: it puts <code>?saved=…</code> in the address.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The list, with a flag in the URL" Url="demo/tempdata/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: a flag in the URL" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Click the link <code>…/net10?saved=99999</code> above the list. It says "Order 99999 saved", but that
            order doesn't exist. Now save a real order and press <b>⟳</b> above the frame. It says "saved" again.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The message lives in the address</h2>
    <p>The list doesn't get a message from the save. It just reads the address:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What the list sees</span>
        <pre class="snip">POST /orders/11078   <span class="dim">→ 302 Location: /orders?saved=11078</span>
GET  /orders?saved=11078   <span class="ok">✓ "Order 11078 saved"</span>
GET  /orders?saved=11078   <span class="dim">(refresh)</span>   <span class="err">"Order 11078 saved" again</span>
GET  /orders?saved=99999   <span class="dim">(a shared link)</span>   <span class="err">"Order 99999 saved", nothing saved</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The list can't tell a redirect from a refresh, a bookmark or a typed link. Whatever is in the address shows
        up every time.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Set the message, read it once</h2>
    <p>
        The edit page writes the message with a <code>[SupplyParameterFromTempData]</code> property. The list reads
        it with <code>ITempData.Get</code>, not with the attribute. If the list read it with the attribute, the
        message would be kept and show again. <code>Get</code> reads it once and then it's gone.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// The edit page</span>
<span class="hl">[SupplyParameterFromTempData(Name = "flash")]</span> public string? Flash { get; set; }

<span class="hl">Flash = $"Order {Id} saved";</span>
Navigation.NavigateTo("orders");

<span class="dim">// The list</span>
<span class="hl">[CascadingParameter] public ITempData? TempData { get; set; }</span>

message = <span class="hl">TempData?.Get("flash")</span> as string;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The redirect stores the message in a cookie. The cookie is encrypted and signed, so a tampered one is
        ignored. When the list reads the message, its response deletes the cookie. You don't need to set anything
        up: <code>AddRazorComponents()</code> already includes TempData.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same list, with TempData" Url="demo/tempdata/orders"
                Label=".NET 11: TempData">
        <Caption>
            Save an order. The list says "Order … saved", with no flag in the address. Press <b>⟳</b> above the
            frame. The message is gone.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two pages behind Fig. 2:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/TempData/EditOrder.razor" Feature="tempdata-ssr">
        <Caption>
            The edit page. Instead of adding <code>?saved=</code> to the redirect, it sets <code>Flash</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/TempData/OrderList.razor" Feature="tempdata-ssr">
        <Caption>
            The list. <code>Get</code> reads the message once, so a refresh shows nothing.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>A URL flag, or your own cookie</h2>
    <p>Here's what each option did in a .NET 10 build. The cookie version is copied from Blazor's built-in login pages:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to say "saved" in .NET 10, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">NavigateTo($"orders?saved={Id}");</pre>
                <span class="why">the list shows a banner when <code>?saved=</code> is there</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a refresh shows it again; <code>?saved=99999</code> shows it with nothing saved</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">Response.Cookies.Append("Status",
    $"Order {Id} saved",
    new() { MaxAge = <span class="err">TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)</span> });</pre>
                <span class="why">the list reads it, then deletes it</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ shows once; but it's plain text anyone can fake, and a slow redirect can outlast 5 seconds</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">[CascadingParameter] ITempData? TempData
[SupplyParameterFromTempData]</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ CS0246: neither type exists in .NET 10</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The cookie works. Drop the <code>MaxAge</code> and encrypt the value, and it's safe. But every app had to
        write that by hand.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>TempData, built in</h2>
    <p>
        Now Blazor does it for you, in an encrypted cookie. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Reading through the attribute?</b> Set the property to null after you read it, or the message shows again. <code>Get</code> is simpler.</li>
        <li><b>Simple values only.</b> Strings, ints, bools, Guids, <code>DateTime</code>s and int enums, plus arrays, lists and dictionaries of those. Anything else throws when TempData is saved, even a <code>decimal</code> like <code>42.50m</code>. Put it in the message string, or store the order id and look it up.</li>
        <li><b>Static SSR pages only.</b> Interactive components have no HTTP request, so <code>ITempData</code> is null and <code>TempData?.Get</code> returns nothing.</li>
        <li><b>A cookie by default.</b> It lasts for the browser session, and the response that reads it deletes it. To keep the values on the server instead, use session. Set <code>TempDataProviderType.SessionStorage</code> in the <code>AddRazorComponents</code> options. Then turn session on with <code>AddDistributedMemoryCache()</code>, <code>AddSession()</code> and <code>UseSession()</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
